  `⟪∇_X Y, Z⟫ = (X⟪Y,Z⟫ + Y⟪Z,X⟫ - Z⟪X,Y⟫ - ⟪Y,[X,Z]⟫ - ⟪Z,[Y,X]⟫ + ⟪X,[Z,Y]⟫)/2`.
If we choose X, Y, Z to be the *coordinate vector fields* in the chart, then the Lie brackets `[∂_i, ∂_j] = 0`, and the derivatives are ordinary partial derivatives of the metric coefficients. So the coordinate Christoffel symbols can be computed precisely by this formula if we can produce, for each `x : E` and `i`, a global (or local) vector field on M that equals `∂_i` near the point and is differentiable.

